  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 157 AM MDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nA more robust surge of Monsoon moisture is still favored to enter \nthe Desert Southwest going into next week, and in turn PoPs continue \nto trend up across the area (mainly western and northern NM). Taking \na look at forecast PWAT anomalies for the majority of next week in \nensemble clustering, all clusters favor areas in western and \nnorthern NM climbing into the 80-90th percentiles in PWATs. Value \nwise, this brings PWATs near and above an inch, especially going \ninto Wednesday and beyond. This agreement between ensemble clusters \nshows higher confidence in the atmosphere being richer in moisture, \nwith a better chance of storms across much of the forecast area. The \npattern makes sense meteorologically too: a slightly weakening high \npressure allowing moisture to seep in underneath and on its slight \nwestern periphery. Translating to our forecast: scattered to \nnumerous thunderstorms appear increasingly likely going into the \nearly and middle parts of next week. Exact coverage of storms will \nlikely change as finer details of the pattern become available in \nshort-term model guidance, but this looks to bring some moisture \nback to areas of the state that surely need it.\n\nOn top of more moisture returning to the area, this (along with the \nhigh pressure weakening slightly) should allow high temperatures to \ndrop a few degrees, bringing them closer to average. We may still be \ndealing with Heat Advisories going into the latter half of the \nweekend for portions of central and western NM, but as several days \nof a higher PWAT atmosphere settle in, we should see temperatures \nreturn closer to normal (high 80s to low 90s, with mid 90s in the \nlowest elevations) through mid next week.",
